[b]Fib Reaction Memory[/b] scores Fibonacci retracement levels of an active reference leg by the confirmed local price reactions that actually happened inside that leg — not by whether price simply crossed the level.

[b]What it does[/b]

Most Fib tools ask "was price ever at 61.8%?" This one asks "did price show a confirmed local reaction near 61.8% while this specific leg was being built?" It locates smaller confirmed pivots inside the current structural A→B swing, measures how far price moved away from each one in ATR terms, and only then checks whether that reaction sits near a standard Fib ratio. The result is a 0–100 Memory Score per level, drawn directly onto the Fib lines themselves — no separate table.

[b]How the reference leg works[/b]

[list]
[*]A→B comes from confirmed structural swing pivots and stays frozen while price pulls back inside it
[*]The leg only changes when a new confirmed pivot extends beyond B, or the pullback invalidates past A
[*]This avoids the common trap where a small counter-pivot silently swaps out the range you're actually trying to read
[/list]

[b]Reaction memory[/b]

[list]
[*]Smaller pivots inside the leg are located and sized in ATR units — weak stalls are filtered out, larger reactions count more
[*]Each qualifying reaction is normalized onto the 0–1 leg range and matched to nearby Fib levels within a configurable radius
[*]Evidence accumulates with a saturating curve, so repeated touches don't produce an unbounded score
[*]Optional recency weighting lets later reactions in the leg count slightly more
[*]Level consumption: each additional test of the same level counts for less than the first, since a repeatedly tested level typically weakens rather than staying equally valid
[*]Broken-level penalty: a reaction counts for less if price later broke decisively past that level anyway — a rejection that held up counts as stronger evidence than one that was later broken through
[*]Hold Rate per level: the % of historical reactions near that level that were never subsequently broken, shown next to the score
[/list]

[b]Reading the levels[/b]

Each Fib line's color and width reflect its Memory Score — gray/thin for little evidence, orange for relevant structure, bold for strong structure. Each level also carries a state — APPROACHING, TESTING, REJECTED, ACCEPTED, or RECLAIMED — based on the live pullback. A highlighted NEXT level marks the strongest-evidence level still ahead of price — not necessarily the geometrically nearest one.

[b]Important[/b]

A high Memory Score means this area showed meaningful historical structure within the current leg. It is not a probability estimate and does not predict whether the level will hold.

